 which of the following is true?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054972 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "126eb637-e5ca-5143-b535-559e40bc4ac8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055905 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055905 #edb/ref 1061 86199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055905 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ea6b6835-3d1a-5b28-9df6-f7c82c95a7a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055905 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055906 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055906 #edb/ref 1061 86200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055906 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "59430871-5d21-53f3-a1dc-d90c496d02fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055906 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055907 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055907 #edb/ref 1061 86204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055907 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b8cc6fa4-8144-59f9-8c08-a90356ab153e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055907 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055908 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055908 #edb/ref 1061 86208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055908 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fbc9e243-fffa-5f93-9ca4-244251573ebd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055908 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055909 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055909 #edb/ref 1061 86209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055909 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1dd5dac0-5f14-54bf-a05b-c4a8f4ce5b61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055909 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055910 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055910 #edb/ref 1061 86212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055910 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "93619eb6-b7dc-54fd-a1e0-a79da641bd64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055910 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055911 #edb/ref 1061 86213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055911 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cdaf05f4-d95a-5b13-bdac-bd7178402145" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055911 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056036 #edb/ref 1064 "A **set** is a collection of objects known as **elements** or **members**.\n\nUsually, the elements of a set have some property in common. For example, a set could be a class at school where the students are the members of the set. The common property is that all the students are members of the class.\n\nTo write a set, we list the elements between two braces (curly brackets). Commas are used to separate the elements.\n\nSo, for example, the set of the primary colors would be written as\n\n$$\nS =\\{\\text{yellow}, \\: \\text{red}, \\: \\text{blue} \\}\n$$\n\nThe elements of this set are $\\text{yellow}$, $\\text{red}$, and $\\text{blue}$.\n\nA set that does not contain any elements is known as the **null set** or **empty set** and is written as either\n$\\emptyset \\quad \\text{or} \\quad \\{\\}$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- *A set may contain other sets as elements.* For instance, the set\n$T = \\{1, \\: 2, \\: 3, \\: 4, \\: \\{5, 6\\}\\}$\ncontains the set $\\{5, 6\\}$ as an element. However, it does *not* contain the elements $5$ and $6$.\n- *Order doesn't matter.* The order in which the elements are listed is irrelevant. For instance, the following sets represent the same thing:\n$A = \\{1, \\: 2, \\: 3 \\} = \\{2, \\: 3, \\: 1\\}$\nIt's usually easier to understand a set if the elements are listed in some order.\n- *Duplicates don't matter.* Having multiple copies of an element does not affect the structure of a set. For example, the following sets represent the same thing:\n$B = \\{m, \\: n, \\: p, \\: q\\} = \\{m, \\: m, \\: m, \\: n, \\: n, \\: p, \\: p, \\: p, \\: q\\}$\nWhen writing a set, we should try to organize it so that each element is listed once only." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056036 #edb/ref 1065 8211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056036 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d42ef3b1-d046-5e5d-b671-e5bb3eee9963" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056036 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056037 #edb/ref 1064 "By convention, sets are denoted by uppercase letters, while lowercase letters refer to elements of the set.\n\nThere is a special notation, $\\in$, for expressing elements of sets. For example, if $a$ is an element of a set $A$, we write\n\n$$\na \\in A\n$$\n\nFor example, for the set $S$, given by\n\n$$\nS = \\{\\text{yellow}, \\text{red}, \\text{blue} \\}\n$$\n\nwe can write\n\n$$\n\\text{yellow}\\in S\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $a$ is *not* an element of $A$, then we write\n\n$$\na \\notin A\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\text{green} \\notin S\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056037 #edb/ref 1065 8212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056037 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2d5957fc-01d1-567b-8834-752b6a1b99a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056037 #edb/ref 1067 "Element Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056038 #edb/ref 1064 "When a set contains only a few elements, we can write them all explicitly.\n\nFor example, the set $A$ containing all positive even integers less than $20$ could be written as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\{2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: 8, \\: 10, \\: 12, \\: 14, \\: 16, \\: 18\\}\n$$\n\nHowever, when a set contains many elements, we can save ourselves the pain of writing out all its elements using an ellipsis, \"$\\ldots$\".\n\nFor example:\n\n- The set $B$ containing all positive even integers less than $200$ can be written as\n$B = \\{2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: \\ldots, \\: 198\\}$.\n- The set $C$ containing *all* positive even integers can be written as\n$C = \\{2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: \\ldots \\}$.\n\nGenerally, an ellipsis means \"and so on up to\" if it's followed by a specific element or \"and so on\" if not.\n\nFinally, note that $A$ and $B$ contain a finite number of elements ($A$ and $B$ contain $9$ and $99$ elements, respectively), while $C$ contains infinitely many elements:\n\n- A set containing a finite number of elements is called a **finite set**.\n- A set containing an infinite number of elements is called an **infinite set**.\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ are finite sets, while $C$ is an infinite set." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056038 #edb/ref 1065 8213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056038 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "eda65aa9-454a-53a2-a1cd-d218730af2b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056038 #edb/ref 1067 "Ellipsis Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
